*Elements of Art. 13(4) - Sufficient Provocation*  
      1. The provocation must be sufficient; 
      2. The provocation must originate from the offended party; and 
      3. The provocation must be immediate to the commission of the crime by the person provoked.  
It is necessary that the provocation originated from the victim. If the provocation originated from a third person, the accused cannot invoke this mitigating circumstance.  
The third element does not allow for a lapse of time. To be appreciated as a mitigating circumstance, the law requires that after the victim provoked the accused, the accused must immediately commit the wrongful act against the victim.

###URBANO v. PEOPLE G.R. NO. 182750 | JANUARY 20, 2009 | VELASCO, JR., J.
### FACTS: 
Victim Brigido Tomelden and Urbano had a heated altercation wherein former hurled insulting remarks at Urbano. The exchange of words led to an exchange of blows. They refused to be pacified and continued throwing fist blows at each other. Urbano then delivered a "lucky punch" on Tomelden's face, which made Tomelden topple down. Rosario brought Tomelden to the Sison Memorial Provincial Hospital, where the attending physician diagnosed Tomelden to be suffering from "brain injury, secondary to mauling to consider cerebral hemorrhage." Later on, he died. Urbano was charged with the crime of Homicide.
### ISSUE: 
W/N sufficient provocation on the part of the victim and lack of intent to commit so grave a wrong should be appreciated (YES)
### RULING: 
YES. The mitigating circumstances of sufficient provocation on the part of the victim and lack of intent to commit so grave a wrong are present. Paragraphs 3 and 4 of Art. 13 of the RPC provide, as follows:  
    Art. 13. Mitigating circumstances. - The following are mitigating circumstances:  
        3. That the offender had no intention to commit so grave a wrong as that committed.  
        4. That sufficient provocation or threat on the part of the offended party immediately preceded the act. x x x
When the law speaks of provocation either as a mitigating circumstance or as an essential element of self-defense, the reference is to an unjust or improper conduct of the offended party capable of exciting, inciting, or irritating anyone. It is not enough that the provocative act be unreasonable or annoying, the provocation must be sufficient to excite one to commit the wrongful act and should immediately precede the act. This third requisite of self-defense is present: (i) when no provocation at all was given to the aggressor; (ii) when, even if provocation was given, it was not sufficient; (iii) when, even if the provocation was sufficient, it was not given by the person defending himself; and (iv) when even if a provocation was given by the person defending himself, it was not proximate and immediate to the act of aggression.
In the instant case, Tomelden's insulting remarks directed at Urbano and uttered immediately before the fist fight constituted sufficient provocation. This is not to mention other irritating statements made by the deceased while they were having beer in Bugallon. Urbano was the one provoked and challenged to a fist fight.
Moreover, the mitigating circumstance that Urbano had no intention to commit so grave a wrong as that committed should also be appreciated in his favor. While intent to kill may be presumed from the fact of the death of the victim, this mitigating factor may still be considered when attendant facts and circumstances so warrant, as in the instant case. Considering that Urbano tried to avoid the fight, being very much smaller than Tomelden. He tried to parry the blows of Tomelden, albeit he was able, during the scuffle, to connect a "lucky punch" that ended the fight. And lest it be overlooked, Urbano helped carry his unconscious co-worker to the office of the LIWAD's general manager. Surely, such gesture cannot reasonably be expected from, and would be unbecoming of, one intending to commit so grave a wrong as killing the victim. A bare-knuckle fight as a means to parry the challenge issued by Tomelden was commensurate to the potential violence Urbano was facing. It was just unfortunate that Tomelden died from that "lucky punch", an eventuality that could have possibly been averted had he had the financial means to get the proper medical attention. Thus, it is clear that the mitigating circumstance of no intention to commit so grave a wrong as that committed must also be appreciated in favor of Urbano while finding him guilty of Homicide. That Urbano landed a lucky punch on Tomelden's face while their co-workers were trying to separate them is a compelling indicium that he never intended so grave a wrong as to kill the victim.
### NOTE
In the case of Urbano vs. People, the SC considered the mitigating circumstances of praeter intentionem and sufficient provocation.  The Court appreciated praeter intentionem since there was a notable disparity between the means employed by the accused in hitting the victim with one "lucky" punch and the resulting felony which produced the death of the victim. 
The Court held that the accused did not intend to commit so grave a wrong, as that of killing the victim.  The Court also considered sufficient provocation. The acts done by the victim were sufficient to stir the accused to commit a wrongful act. The wrongful act was proportionate to the provocation since it merely ensued into a confrontation and later, into a fist fight. Therefore, there was sufficient provocation which originated from the victim.

### MIRANDA v. PEOPLE G.R. NO. 234528 | JANUARY 23, 2019 | REYES, JR., J.
### FACTS: 
Pilo and Damaso were already on their way home from a party when they passed by the house of Miranda and they threw stones at the latter's home. While Pilo was on his way home, Miranda suddenly went outside and started hacking Pilo. He hit Pilo' s right forehead. Again, Miranda tried to hit Pilo, but the latter parried the attack with his left arm. Miranda was charged with the crime of Frustrated Homicide. Miranda contends that while he admits that he hacked Pilo, his acts where done in self-defense.
### ISSUE 
W/N self-defense should be appreciated (NO)
### RULING: 
NO. The SC held that although the justifying circumstance of self-defense may not be appreciated in the present case, the mitigating circumstance of sufficient provocation may be appreciated.  
In *Gotis v. People*, the Court held that while an act cannot be considered an unlawful aggression for the purpose of self-defense, the same act may be regarded as sufficient provocation for the purpose of mitigating the crime. "As a mitigating circumstance, sufficient provocation is any unjust or improper conduct or act of the victim adequate enough to excite a person to commit a wrong, which is accordingly proportionate in gravity." The victim must have committed a prior act that incited or irritated the accused. Likewise, in order to be mitigating, the provocation must be sufficient and should immediately precede the act.  
Although Pilo' s act of hurling stones may not be regarded as an unlawful aggression, admittedly, however, such deed was vexatious, improper, and enough to incite Miranda into anger. The fact that Miranda was stirred to rage was understandable considering that his wife and daughter were at his home, and were peacefully having supper when Pilo threw the stones.  
In fact, in a long line of cases, the Court considered that although there may have been no unlawful aggression on the part of the victim, if the latter was, nonetheless, deemed to have given sufficient provocation, then the accused's liability shall be mitigated. Such acts which were deemed vexatious range from the victim's act of challenging the accused's family while armed with a bolo; or thrusting a bolo at the accused while threatening to kill him, with the lives of the accused's wife and children placed in peril; and the victim attempting to hack the accused. Pilo's act falls within this range.
###NOTE: 
In the case of Miranda vs. People, the SC held that the act of the victim in throwing stones at the house of the accused cannot be considered as unlawful aggression. Thus, the accused's invocation of self- defense has no merit. It is not unlawful aggression because based on the facts, the stones only hit the roof and the door of the house. Therefore, the said act of throwing the stones did not place the life and limb of the accused and his family in actual and imminent danger. However, while self-defense cannot be appreciated, sufficient provocation can be considered. According to the SC, the victim's act of throwing the stones annoyed, vexed or irritated the accused. Therefore, there is sufficient provocation as a mitigating circumstance.

###5.  IMMEDICATE VINDICATION OF A GRAVE OFFENSE 
*Elements of Art. 13(5) - Immediate Vindication of a Grave Offense*  
        1. That there be a grave offense done to the accused or to any member of his family; and 
        2. That the said commission of the crime was done in immediate vindication of the grave offense.  
Otherwise stated, the grave offense must be the proximate cause of the commission of the crime.  
Under the second element, the word "immediate" allows for a lapse of time. The reason given by the SC is that there was erroneous translation of the Spanish Codigo Penal. In the Spanish Codigo Penal, the word "proxima" was used, which was translated into "immediate" under the RPC. Under the second element, it suffices that the grave offense is the proximate cause for the commission of the crime.

*Sufficient Provocation Vis-à-vis Vindication of a Grave Offense*
*Sufficient Provocation*
      - Does not allow for a lapse of time. 
      - To be appreciated as a mitigating circumstance, the law requires that after the victim provokes the accused, the accused must immediately act against the victim.

*Vindication of a Grave Offense*
      - Allows of a lapse of time.
      - It suffices that the grave offense is the proximate cause for the commission of the crime. 

























